<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lower Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">3400 BCE – 3100 BCE (approx.) · capital Buto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The predynastic Nile Delta, remembered by later Egyptians as a kingdom of the red crown centred on Buto before unification with the south.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Later Egyptian tradition held that before unification the Delta formed a kingdom of its own, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a> (Pe and Dep) and the red crown as its emblem. Archaeologically the region was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto-maadi-culture">Buto-Maadi culture</a> of the 4th millennium BCE, with settlements at Buto, Maadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-el-farkha">Tell el-Farkha</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Whether the Delta was ever a single kingdom is debated; many scholars see a cluster of chiefdoms and trading towns gradually absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> culture spreading from the south around 3400 to 3300 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-stone">Palermo Stone</a> lists names of early northern kings, but they cannot be checked. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a> was home to the cobra goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a>, and Maadi traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, wine and oil with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The north was brought under southern rule around 3100 BCE, remembered as its conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a> and the founding of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a>. The red crown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a> survived as emblems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> in royal regalia and titles for three thousand years.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 3400 BCE – 3100 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:0.00%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>3400 BCE – 3100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd>35th century BCE – 32nd century BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buto</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Predynastic Lower Egypt, Kingdom of Buto</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-3250&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lower-egypt" aria-label="Kingdom of Lower Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5474 1041 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lower Egypt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5794 1297L5813 1344"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5813 1344h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5794 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5794 1297h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Buto. <a href="/atlas?year=-3250&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lower-egypt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

